  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "Prove that if $n$ is an integer greater than 1, then $\\log_n(n+1)$ is irrational.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Irrational.\n\nA rational value would give $(n+1)^q=n^p$, but the two sides have opposite parity.",
      "worked": "Suppose $\\log_n(n+1)$ is rational. Since $n>1$, write it as $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p,q$ are positive integers. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_n(n+1)&=\\frac pq\\\\n+1&=n^{p/q}\\\\(n+1)^q&=n^p.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $n$ is even, the left-hand side is odd and the right-hand side is even. If $n$ is odd, the left-hand side is even and the right-hand side is odd. Both cases contradict the equality. Therefore $\\log_n(n+1)$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
